WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:02.360
[Soft piano music]

00:00:02.740 --> 00:00:09.480
[Porter] I study proteins and proteins have been
thought to have one structure that has one function or fold.

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:13.260
 I'm studying this
group of proteins called fold switching proteins.

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:18.260
 So, they can actually change
their structures and their functions in

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:22.350
response to changes in the cell. So you
can kind of imagine fold switching

00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:26.640
proteins are like a transformer where in
one case the protein is like a robot

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:31.199
that does one thing and then in another
case, in response to changes in our

00:00:31.199 --> 00:00:34.530
bodies, it becomes a car and can do
something else.

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:41.500
And an advantage to this is it can
respond really quickly to changes in our bodies.

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:45.180
Back in high school, I did not
imagine myself being a scientist at all.

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:48.360
Before going to college, I did kind of
fall in love with math like when I took

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:53.309
calculus. I was like wow this is so cool!
It was like the first time I realized

00:00:53.309 --> 00:00:56.780
that math could be useful for something
beyond balancing my checkbook. 

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:04.080
At the end of my sophomore year of college my dad
was diagnosed with stage 4 Lymphoma. 

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:08.850
He went through multiple rounds of chemo
and it was just a really hard process

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:14.120
just watching that happen and and like
thinking I wonder if there's a better way.

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:18.780
Some of the proteins that I'm
working on that actually do this

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:24.530
phenomenon called fold switching are
actually associated with diseases so

00:01:24.530 --> 00:01:30.960
cancer, Alzheimer's, autoimmune disease
bacterial and viral infections. 

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:39.460
If by the end of my life even
one successful treatment was made based on this that would be amazing.

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:44.180
 
NLM has a really strong track record in computation.

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:47.480
There are a lot of excellent
scientists here and so I thought it

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:52.110
would be great to be able to work with
them. I'm also like really grateful to

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:57.329
have the freedom to pursue what I want
to do and I'm really like happy to be

00:01:57.329 --> 00:02:02.039
here and be able to like take chances
that I probably couldn't do in most

00:02:02.039 --> 00:02:04.909
other environments.

